Review round 1: bundle the review launcher
Decision pending. Four P2 findings remain open. No fixes have been started.

Purpose
Workers should use one reliable script to launch each reviewer or fixer, with
the correct model, safe prompt passing, clear failures, and separate review
files for each task. This removes repeated hand-built commands and preserves
earlier reviews when a task slot is reused.

Implementation and review so far
The implementation is committed as 55c9b04f9160240e454ecab5039e26d86e0bf815
on fm/fm-review-round-script. The independent first review reproduced four
problems. No fix round has run. The reviewer restored all experiments and
reran the affected tests successfully. A final review has not run, and this
branch has not been marked ready to land.

1. Logs can stand in for an empty review (R1-1, P2)
Example: a fake runtime prints only "runtime ready; no assistant reply" to
its error stream and exits successfully. The launcher reports success even
though there is no review. A reply consisting only of spaces also succeeds.
The script currently tests whether its combined output file has any bytes.
Likelihood: conditional on a runtime returning success without a meaningful
reply; this was proved with fakes, but its frequency in real runtimes was not
measured. It can affect any worker and allow an incomplete review to be treated
as complete. The downstream impact depends on what the omitted review missed.
Fix cost: approximately 20-35 minutes to distinguish a role reply from runtime
logs for both runtimes and add tests for diagnostic-only and whitespace output.

2. Replacing an old launch command can erase an extra rule (R1-2, P2)
Example input: "Before launching claude -p /adv-review-loop, run the required
unit tests." Generated instructions replace the whole sentence with a launcher
pointer. The requirement to run those tests disappears.
Likelihood: deterministic whenever a configured sentence contains both command
tokens and another instruction. The prevalence of such configuration was not
measured. It affects both ordinary ship instructions and promoted scouts, and
could remove a home's required test or approval rule.
Fix cost: approximately 10-20 minutes to restrict replacement to actual launch
commands while preserving surrounding policy, with tests for mixed prose.

3. Supported task names containing periods cannot start review (R1-3, P2)
Example: the existing task-name validator accepts "release.v2", but the new
launcher's initialization rejects it. Generated instructions pass that task
name straight through, so the review cannot start.
Likelihood: certain for these otherwise supported task names; unaffected names
use letters, numbers, dashes and underscores. Affected workers stop before
review, delaying completion rather than silently approving work.
Fix cost: approximately 5-10 minutes to align validation with the existing
task-name contract and add a dotted-name regression.

4. Tests miss an accidental second role launch (R1-4, P2)
The current script invokes the role once. The reviewer deliberately duplicated
that invocation, and all three affected test files still passed. The fake
runtimes record the final arguments, but do not check the number of calls.
Likelihood: a future maintenance regression; duplicate execution is not present
in the committed implementation. If introduced, it would spend extra review
capacity and could run the same fixer twice.
Fix cost: approximately 5-10 minutes to record every fake-runtime invocation and
require exactly one call for each requested role round.

Open questions for firstmate
- The role definition's handoff instructions may still use a shared root file.
  The generated input identifies the previous handoff, but does not specify a
  new task-specific destination. The reviewer did not run a real fixer to prove
  what it would write. Explicitly naming that destination would likely take
  another 5-10 minutes and fits the accepted task-file isolation requirement.
- The reviewer reported three long-running synthetic probes with very large
  repeated-placeholder policies. The same substitution code exists on main;
  no regression caused by this branch was established. Current completion of
  those probes is unverified. Do not expand this task into a performance change
  on this evidence alone, and do not kill processes without separate authority.

Evidence and checks
